<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Formigny</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1450">1450</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Formigny was fought on 15 April 1450 in Normandy, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. A French army under the Count of Clermont, joined by Breton troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-iii-duke-of-brittany">Arthur de Richemont</a>, destroyed an English relief force under Sir Thomas Kyriell. The victory led to the French reconquest of Normandy, completed with the fall of Cherbourg in August 1450.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-formigny.jpg" alt="Battle of Formigny" width="320" height="295" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Formigny.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Arthur de Richemont, constable of France and later Duke Arthur III, helped reform the French royal army and shared in the victory at the <b>Battle of Formigny</b> in 1450.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Recovery came with Joan of Arc's relief of Orléans in 1429 and with Charles VII's reforms, which in 1445 created the compagnies d'ordonnance, the first permanent royal cavalry, and used artillery to win at <b>Formigny</b> in 1450 and Castillon in 1453.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Military</a></li></ul></section>
